By target: `diagram-core` 279, `new-module` 697, `ds-component` 125, `diagram-canvas` 77, `graph` 59. By priority: `P0` 272, `P1` 724, `P2` 241. All figures are reproducible with `node tools/re-bpmn-js/enumerate.mjs` (summary printed to stdout, full detail in `docs/re/bpmn-js-inventory.json`) and cross-checked by `node tools/re-bpmn-js/verify.mjs` (§3.2).

Reading of the result. The estate to rebuild is dominated by four blocks: the semantic metamodel (`moddle-type` 174 = 163 types + 11 enumerations, owned by A′ generation, §7), the event/interaction surface (`event` 208 + `module` 92, i.e. the didi module wiring and its EventBus contract), the service API (`api` 437: every didi service class surface, including `Clipboard`, `CopyPaste`, `Palette`, `ContextPad`, `PopupMenu`, `SearchPad`, `Keyboard`, `Rules`, `BpmnReplace` and the factories, alongside the viewer/modeler facades), and the menu atoms (`replace-menu` 74 + `popup-menu` 17 + `context-pad` 19 + `palette` 15 = 125 chrome entries). The genuinely-started blocks are the transactional core (13/33 commands partial), the headless scene/camera (15 API + 22 event entries partial) and connection rendering (5/58 renderer entries partial). Nothing is `covered`: parity work starts from partial mechanisms, not from finished behaviour — any plan that assumes otherwise understates every lot in §9.

### 4.3 Cross-checks against independent counts

- Moddle types: 163 counted here (137 `bpmn` + 6 `bpmndi` + 11 `di` + 7 `dc` + 2 `bioc`; 27 abstract, 136 concrete) + 3 `color` (source-gap, §3.3) = 166 types, matching the A′ spec's registry measurement (166 types, 27 abstract; `spec/SPEC_EVOL_GD_BPMN_MODDLE_COVERAGE.md` §3.1). The 11 enumerations (9 `bpmn` from `RE:bpmn-moddle/resources/bpmn/json/bpmn.json:2822`, 2 `bpmndi` from line 158) are additional `moddle-type` entries (174 total), not types, and do not change the A′ type total. The counts were produced by different routes (JSON descriptors vs runtime registry) and agree once `color` is accounted for.
- Renderer entries (58 = 39 `handlers` including `label`, 12 `eventIconRenderers`, 7 `taskMarkerRenderers`) vs moddle types (163): the renderer draws *diagram* shapes/connections, not metamodel types; the gap is expected — most moddle types (event definitions, artefacts' internals) have no dedicated glyph and are rendered through their flow-node handler plus markers/icons. The per-type rendering obligation is therefore: every concrete flow element type must resolve to a handler + marker/icon variant, which the harness checks by fixture (§8), not by counting handlers.
- Commands (33) vs `diagram-core` commands (18 at `WT:packages/diagram-core/src/commands.ts:149`): different vocabularies (diagram operations vs semantic transactions). §5.2 defines the mapping contract; no numeric parity is claimed between the two lists.

## 5. Target architecture — renderer-independent contract

The capitalisation requirement: **no behaviour in this section names SVG, DOM, WebGL, xyflow or any framework.** A behaviour is specified as a contract over five planes; each route in §6 is an implementation of the same planes. Replaying the spec on the WebGL graph then means re-implementing planes 4–5 only.

### 5.1 Plane 1 — Semantic model (owner: `diagram-core`, per A′)

The model is the A′ model (§7): `SemanticDocument` + `ViewDocument`(s) in `diagram-core`, with BPMN profiles *generated* from the bpmn-moddle descriptors. bpmn-js's runtime object graph (`businessObject`, `$parent`, DI elements) is a *reference reading* of that model, never a second authority. Contract obligations:

- Every `moddle-type` entry (163 types + 11 enumerations + `color` 3 types) maps to a generated profile type, a generated enumeration value set, or to preserved unknown-namespace content (counted separately, per A′).
- Containment, references (including `isMany`, `isReference`, virtual/replaces/redefines handling) follow the A′ mapping rules; connectors (`SequenceFlow`, `MessageFlow`, associations, data associations) are entities with typed references, projected to edges for display (A′ §5 rationale).
- The exchange form is moddle JSON (F1) end-to-end; XML only at the edges (import/export, inside tests), per A′ and the migration plan's "one JSON model" rule.

### 5.2 Plane 2 — Commands and transactions (owner: `diagram-core` + a new interaction layer)

Each `command` entry becomes a contract: *preconditions (plane 3 rules) → transaction → effects + emitted events (plane 5 contract names) → logical inverse (undo) → redo*. `diagram-core` already provides the transaction machinery (apply/inverse/revision-conflict, `WT:packages/diagram-core/src/commands.ts:149`); what parity adds is the BPMN command vocabulary on top of it:

- The 21 diagram-js generic commands (`shape.*`, `connection.*`, `elements.*`, `label.create`, `spaceTool`, …) map to occurrence/relation transactions; the 11 bpmn-js commands (`lane.*`, `element.updateProperties`, `element.updateModdleProperties`, `element.updateLabel`, `element.setColor`, `canvas.updateRoot`, `group.updateRefs`, `id.updateClaim`, `dataStore.updateContainment`) map to entity/attribute transactions. The inventory's `command` entries carry this mapping per item (`target` + `behaviour`).
- Undo/redo, command-stack dirty state and `commandStack.*` event semantics (including the dynamic suffixes, §3.3) are part of the contract: an implementation that mutates without an inverse fails parity even if the end state matches.
- Behaviours attached to commands in bpmn-js (the 44 behaviour files under `RE:bpmn-js/lib/features/modeling/behavior/`, excluding `index.js`; counted with `find … -name "*.js" ! -name "index.js" | wc -l` → 44, e.g. boundary-event, label, lane and subprocess-plane behaviours) are *effects of* the command contract, enumerated indirectly through the `command`/`event` families; each behaviour file must be accounted for by the lot that implements its command (checked in lot acceptance, §9).

### 5.3 Plane 3 — Rules (owner: `diagram-core` validation + interaction layer)

Each `rule` entry is a pure predicate over (model, geometry context) returning boolean or a typed result (`{ type: 'bpmn:MessageFlow' }`, `{ replacements: [...] }`, `'attach'`, …), exactly as `BpmnRules` returns them (`RE:bpmn-js/lib/features/rules/BpmnRules.js`, `init` and the `can*` functions). Rules never mutate. The BPMN well-formedness matrix they encode (sequence/message/data flow eligibility, boundary attach, drop/insert/replace, resize, copy) is specified by the reference implementation's *observable verdicts*; the harness probes verdicts, it does not port the code (clean room, §10). Rules that diagram-js provides generically (`elements.move/create` partial today) are completed to BPMN specificity here.

### 5.4 Plane 4 — Abstract rendering (owner: `@sentropic/graph` on route 1; xyflow on route 2)

Rendering is specified as: *(scene: typed nodes/edges/labels with geometry) → (draw list: shapes, paths, markers, icons, text) at a camera*. The 58 `renderer` entries (39 handlers including `label`, 12 event-icon renderers, 7 task-marker renderers) define the required draw vocabulary: per handler, the shape outline, fill/stroke, marker set (e.g. message/timer/signal glyphs inside events), icons (task type glyphs), connection line style (solid/dashed/dotted, arrowheads), and label placement. `@sentropic/graph` today provides the substrate — typed buffers, generic shape codes (`WT:packages/graph/src/shape-geometry.ts`, `shapeCode`), edge routing including octilinear (`WT:packages/graph/src/renderer.ts:1180` and `render-geometry`) — but no BPMN glyph set; the lot in §9 adds a BPMN draw layer *consuming* `render-geometry` as the single geometry source (the discipline `diagram-canvas` already applies for hit↔render consistency). Text measurement (bpmn-js `TextRenderer`) is part of this plane: a font-metric source must be decided (open point, §11); `diagram-canvas` currently defers labels for exactly this reason.

### 5.5 Plane 5 — Interaction and chrome (owner: `diagram-canvas` extension + DS components + new editor module)

- **Scene/hit substrate (exists)**: camera transform, scene frame, paint order, point/region hit test (`WT:packages/diagram-canvas/src/scene.ts:168`, `WT:packages/diagram-canvas/src/hit.ts:85`). Parity extends it with selection state, marquee/lasso, drag/connect/create lifecycles, bendpoint editing, resize, space tool, snapping (grid + element), auto-place/auto-resize, align/distribute, ordering (z), overlays, drilldown (subprocess planes), label editing (direct editing), copy-paste, search/search-pad, hand/lasso tool management and keyboard handling — each traceable to `module`/`event`/`keyboard` inventory entries.
- **Chrome (new DS components)**: palette (15), context pad (19), replace menu (74 options), popup menus (17 items: 3 provider registrations, 6 header entries, 6 align entries, 2 distribute entries), properties interaction surfaces. These are DS components consuming the planes above; they own no model logic. The standard-E rendering-by-JSON work (web components), currently in specification, is the intended host contract for this chrome; this spec does not pre-empt its decisions.
- **Events as contract**: the 208 `event` entries are the observable seams between planes (e.g. `element.hover`, `shape.move.*`, `commandStack.changed`, `import.render.*`, `selection.changed`). An implementation emits/compares these names (or a documented translation of them) — the harness compares event logs, so silently renaming an event is a parity failure unless the translation is declared in the lot. Reference APIs that return DOM/SVG nodes (`Canvas.getGraphics`, `getLayer`, `BaseViewer.saveSVG` reading the live viewport) have no literal counterpart in this contract: they map to abstract draw-list handles and to SVG synthesised from the scene (§5.7), never to DOM parity.

### 5.6 What stays out of the contract

Framework idioms (React/Svelte props, didi injection, xyflow node types as an *authoring* format) are route implementation details. The persisted form is A′ state + F1 JSON at the boundary; the interaction state (selection, drag previews, open menus) is ephemeral and never persisted.

### 5.7 Route adapters, camera and explicit Bounds

The two routes of §6 are adapters over the same scene contract; neither may become a second model.

- **Adapter contract.** Input to either adapter is the scene (typed nodes/edges/labels with `dc:Bounds` geometry, paint order, camera) plus the interaction intents of plane 5; output is the draw list of §5.4 and hit results in scene coordinates. The xyflow adapter (route 2, step A) maps scene nodes/edges to xyflow nodes/edges and translates xyflow callbacks into contract events (§5.5); its DOM hit geometry is an adapter detail and is discarded at capitalisation. The `@sentropic/graph` adapter (route 1, step B) maps the same scene to typed buffers through `render-geometry` as the single geometry source, so render and hit can never diverge. Planes 1–3 have no adapter: they are shared code on both routes.
- **Camera.** The persisted camera keeps the `diagram-canvas` reading: `camera.x, camera.y` are the world point drawn at the view origin, `camera.zoom` is CSS pixels per world unit (`WT:packages/diagram-canvas/src/transform.ts:27`). `@sentropic/graph` label layout uses a centre-based reading (`(wx - camera.x) * zoom + width / 2`, recorded at `WT:packages/diagram-canvas/README.md:62` for `WT:packages/graph/src/gitflow-labels.ts`). The conversion lives only inside the graph adapter — centre = origin + (viewport size / 2) / zoom per axis — and never changes persisted state; the harness compares viewboxes in the persisted reading on both routes.
- **Explicit Bounds.** DI geometry is authoritative: every node carries its `dc:Bounds { x, y, width, height }` verbatim (task `100×80`, expanded subprocess, pool/lane rectangles, rotated pool/lane labels, double and dashed outlines). The graph draw layer must therefore accept explicit width/height per node, overriding the text-fitted sizing of `boxDimensions` (`WT:packages/graph/src/render-geometry.ts:173`, which derives width from the label and takes height from the caller). Text fitting remains a fallback for nodes without DI bounds only; multi-waypoint polylines and sub-glyph vectors (markers/icons of §5.4) are draw-list primitives, not node sizes.


## 6. Two routes, compared without a definitive commitment

Both routes implement §5 identically at planes 1–3 (model, commands, rules). They differ at planes 4–5 and use the adapter contract of §5.7. D7′ authorises xyflow in published DS packages under the qualification conditions recalled in §7, so route 2 is legal; legality is not a recommendation. [JUDGMENT] The table states, per family, what xyflow (`@xyflow/react` 12.12.0 / `@xyflow/svelte`, sources at `RE:xyflow/`) provides off the shelf and what must still be built; "free" means shipped and maintained upstream, not zero-integration-cost.

| Family (entries) | Route 1 — direct on `@sentropic/graph` (WebGL) | Route 2 — xyflow first, then graph |
|---|---|---|
| module (92) | Build the editor module set on `diagram-canvas` + new modules. Nothing free; full control of the event contract. | xyflow provides viewport/drag/connect/selection primitives (its `xypanzoom`, `xydrag`, `xyhandle` under `RE:xyflow/packages/system/src/`); the bpmn-js module semantics (behaviours, drilldown, ordering…) still to build as xyflow plugins/custom nodes. |
| command (33) | Implement on `diagram-core` transactions (§5.2). Same work on both routes. | Identical (plane 2 is route-independent). xyflow's own change events must be adapted into transactions — an adapter to write and keep honest (no direct xyflow state mutation). |
| rule (13) | Pure predicates in the interaction layer. Same on both routes. | Identical; xyflow `isValidConnection` is a hook, not the BPMN matrix. |
| palette (15) / context-pad (19) / replace-menu (74) / popup-menu (17) | DS components over our scene/hit substrate. | DS components over xyflow-rendered nodes (toolbar/panel idioms exist: `Panel`, `NodeToolbar` under `RE:xyflow/packages/react/src/`); menu atoms and BPMN logic unchanged — xyflow saves chrome plumbing, not BPMN content. |
| keyboard (30) | Implement on the canvas focus model; full control of chord/modifier/text-input rules. | Partially free (xyflow ships key handling for delete/pan/zoom/selection); BPMN chords (`SHORTCUTS_KEY_BINDINGS`) and editor-action wiring still to build. |
| event (208) | Our EventBus contract mirrors the reference names; harness compares directly. | An event-translation layer is required (xyflow callbacks → contract events). Permanent tax on every harness comparison. |
| config (41) | Editor options designed against §5; `viewer.*`-shaped facade (§5, `api`). | xyflow props cover viewport/interaction options; BPMN options (moddle extensions, renderer config, feature toggles) still ours. Two option surfaces to reconcile. |
| renderer (58) | BPMN draw layer on graph buffers/geometry (WebGL2 + Canvas2D). One geometry source for render + hit. Highest build cost; no intermediate renderer to discard. | xyflow renders DOM/SVG custom nodes: BPMN glyphs as SVG components — faster to first pixels, but the whole draw layer is rewritten for WebGL at capitalisation, and hit geometry lives in the DOM, not in `render-geometry`. |
| moddle-type (174) | A′ generation into `diagram-core`. Identical on both routes. | Identical. |
| api (437) | Facade over planes 1–5 (`importXML`-shaped import via F1 at the edge, `saveXML`/`saveSVG`-shaped export, canvas operations, plus every didi service surface). | Same facade; internally delegates viewport ops to xyflow until capitalisation. Facade insulates consumers from the swap — if the facade leaks xyflow types, the swap cost returns. |
| example (26) | Each example is an acceptance scenario (§8); framework/embedding examples re-hosted on DS chrome. | Same scenarios; `embedding`/`theming`/`minimap` examples map more directly onto xyflow idioms (MiniMap/Controls/Background ship under `RE:xyflow/packages/react/src/additional-components/`). |

**Trade-off summary [JUDGMENT].** Route 2 reaches a demonstrable BPMN vertical sooner (viewport, minimap, controls, node chrome) and de-risks interaction engineering, at the price of a throwaway draw/hit layer and a permanent event/option translation until capitalisation. Route 1 pays the interaction cost once, keeps one geometry source from day one, and has no swap event. What route 2 does *not* save: planes 1–3 (the largest blocks: 174 + 33 + 13 entries plus behaviour effects), the menu atoms (125 entries), or the harness. The decision gate is therefore not "which is cheaper overall" but "is an early vertical worth a disposable plane-4/5 implementation" — an owner decision, prepared (not taken) by lot L0/L1 measurements in §9 (CSP spike already exists for both engines under `WT:tools/csp-spike/` and `WT:tools/xyflow-csp-probe/`; bpmn-js under strict CSP measured a violation for xyflow at load — see the migration plan's CSP gate, still to measure for the chosen route).

**JointJS note (owner request, third leg).** JointJS enters as a *reference* (186 catalogued applications, `WT:docs/graph-dataviz-reference-catalog.md`), not as a runtime dependency: its role in this programme is scenario coverage (which editor applications must our contract express?), feeding `example`-style acceptance scenarios. A mechanical JointJS inventory, if wanted, reuses §3's method against `RE:joint/` in a separate lot; no JointJS code or API shape is adopted.

## 7. Articulation with A′ (semantic model) and with bpmn-canvas

- **Authority.** The semantic model is `diagram-core` under A′ (`spec/SPEC_EVOL_GD_BPMN_MODDLE_COVERAGE.md`, on `main` since #164, `96fc690b`): profiles generated from the bpmn-moddle descriptors, F1 moddle JSON as the exchange pivot, bijection `moddle JSON ↔ DiagramState` proven in a separate private package with bpmn-moddle as a pinned test dependency. This parity spec *consumes* that model; it does not re-decide it. In particular, A′ gate G3 (no `bpmn@2` document persisted outside fixtures before the multi-view bijection passes) binds every lot in §9.
- **Third-party runtimes (D7′).** Decision D7′ (A′ spec §1.2; `spec/SPEC_EVOL_DS_JSON_RENDER_STANDARD.md` §18.4, on `main` since #168) revises D7: `elkjs` and `xyflow` are allowed in published DS manifests under the qualification conditions of `docs/a0-csp-dependency-qualification.md`; bpmn-js is allowed only through `bpmn-canvas`, in the elements package, its bpmn.io watermark governed by the Camunda licence; bpmn-moddle stays a test dependency only, with no runtime XML I/O in any DS package. Consequences for this spec: (a) route 2 (xyflow) is legal under those conditions; (b) the clean-room parity implementation of §5/§9 never imports bpmn-js or diagram-js — the migration plan's step A (xyflow + bpmn-js vertical inside `bpmn-canvas`, `WT:docs/graph-dataviz-migration-plan.md:7,11`) keeps its own status and is explicitly superseded, for DS parity implementation only, by this spec; the two must not be read as authorising each other's dependency.
- **What bpmn-js is here.** A behavioural reference only: the source of observable editing/rendering verdicts (rules, commands, menus, rendering, events). It is never linked, copied or redistributed by the target implementation (clean room, §10).
- **What bpmn-canvas remains.** The production BPMN editor (bpmn-js based, separate repository) until parity is *proven* by the harness (§8) at the gate defined in §9. No DS package depends on bpmn-canvas; the F1 shape alignment items (A′ §4.4, F1-A1…A6) remain `unknown` until bpmn-canvas publishes F1, and every lot touching unknown-namespace retention inherits that status.
- **Input.** Parity fixtures enter as F1 moddle JSON (per A′); XML fixtures enter only through the test-side bpmn-moddle conversion, mirroring A′'s rule that XML exists at the edges inside tests.

## 8. Differential parity harness

Implementation (Lot L0): the harness described in this section is built as the single shared harness in [tools/re-harness/README.md](../tools/re-harness/README.md) (fixture schemas, comparators, coverage verifier, frozen contracts). Read that README for the runner status per library; this section remains the normative design.

### 8.1 Principle

The same inputs are played on the reference (bpmn-js 18.31.0 + diagram-js 15.28.1, pinned sources) and on the target implementation; outputs are compared. A behaviour is `covered` (in the strong, post-implementation sense) only when its differential scenario passes — the inventory's static `status` (§4) is the *starting* coverage, the harness verdict is the *proven* coverage quoted by lot gates (§9). [JUDGMENT] This two-word use is deliberate: static status says where we start, harness verdict says what we may claim.

### 8.2 Inputs

- **Model fixtures**: BPMN files covering every concrete `moddle-type` and every `renderer` handler (including the MIWG corpus files used by A′, event-definition variants, lanes/pools, data objects/stores, groups, text annotations, collapsed/expanded subprocesses, event subprocesses, transactions). Stored as XML (reference-native) + derived F1 JSON (target-native, via the A′ codec under test).
- **Interaction scenarios**: scripted sequences (create via palette/context-pad, drag, connect, reconnect, bendpoint move, resize, space tool, align/distribute, copy-paste, replace via menu, label edit, keyboard chords, drilldown in/out, undo/redo chains). Each scenario names the inventory entries it exercises; the union over scenarios must cover every `P0`/`P1` entry (checked mechanically against the inventory, same style as `verify.mjs`).

### 8.3 Comparison points (in order of strength)

1. **Resulting model**: F1 moddle JSON, canonicalised (sorted keys; DI floats compared with tolerance, §8.4). Strongest signal; catches semantic drift that pixels hide.
2. **Event log**: ordered contract event names + payload keys (values compared where deterministic: ids are remapped through a declared id-correspondence, since creation order may legitimately differ).
3. **Geometry**: element bounds, waypoints, label bounds, viewbox — numeric, with tolerances.
4. **Pixels**: screenshot diff as a *secondary* signal for the renderer family only (font/AA differences make it unfit as a primary gate).

### 8.4 Tooling and tolerances [JUDGMENT]

- Browser driver: Chrome headless via `playwright-core` (present in the repository lockfile; the graph golden harness already drives Chrome via CDP — `WT:packages/graph/tests/golden/`). Both implementations run in the same harness page shell, same viewport, same device pixel ratio, same font set. The reference oracle cannot run in pure Node: bpmn-js/diagram-js need a real SVG/Canvas2D engine (`getBBox` at `RE:bpmn-js/lib/draw/BpmnRenderer.js:1829`, `getPointAtLength` at line 1795, matrix creation in `RE:diagram-js/lib/core/Canvas.js`, 2D canvas colour normalisation in `RE:bpmn-js/lib/features/modeling/cmd/SetColorHandler.js`), which is why upstream tests run under Karma with `ChromeHeadless` (`RE:bpmn-js/package.json:25`; `RE:bpmn-js/test/config/karma.unit.js:12`). Daily CI therefore either drives the live reference in headless Chromium, or replays versioned JSON oracle fixtures recorded from that browser run against `diagram-core`/`diagram-canvas` in pure Node — never a Node-only live reference.
- Tolerances: geometry ±1 CSS px for bounds/waypoints (rounding); label bounds join the ±1 px comparison only once a deterministic font metric is pinned (the reference default is `Arial, sans-serif` at 12 px, `RE:bpmn-js/lib/draw/TextRenderer.js:40` with `DEFAULT_FONT_SIZE` at line 8 — the pinned metric table or measurement bridge is the §11 decision required before L5); until then text metrics are excluded from numeric comparison. Pixel diff threshold per-fixture with a stored baseline and a failure gallery (no global percentage pulled from the air — each threshold is set when its fixture is added and reviewed with it).
- Determinism: fixed seeds, animations/reduced-motion forced off, timers controlled; scenarios that cannot be made deterministic are compared on model+events only and named as such.

### 8.5 What is not compared

Pixel-perfect glyph rasterisation (compared geometrically + by marker/icon presence), upstream bugs (a reference bug is recorded as a *deviation entry* with its source `path:line`, never reproduced silently), performance (measured separately; parity is functional), and bpmn-js extension points with no DS counterpart decision yet (properties-panel ecosystem examples — see §11).

## 9. Lot plan (ordered, with dependencies, gates and cost judgement)

Costs are orders of magnitude in person-weeks for one experienced engineer, stated as judgement [JUDGMENT], calibrated on the inventory sizes — not estimates. Each gate is expressed as proven coverage (harness verdicts, §8.1) over the lot's inventory entries; a lot is done when its gate passes *and* `verify.mjs` still passes. Dependencies: `A′` = the A′ programme lots (G0/G3 gates there); `E` = the standard-E (rendering by JSON) specification, for chrome hosting only.

| Lot | Content (inventory families/keys) | Depends on | Gate (proven coverage) | Cost [JUDGMENT] |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| L0 — Harness & fixtures | Differential harness (§8), fixture corpus, id-correspondence, scenario→entry coverage checker; CSP re-measure for the route decision input | — | Harness runs the reference against itself green (self-parity), including undo chains | 3–5 |
| L1 — Model completion (A′ consumption) | `moddle-type` 174 (163 types + 11 enumerations; +`color` when its source lands), F1 codec consumption, preserved-extension retention. XML export stays at the test edge (A′); `saveSVG` is not a codec deliverable — it belongs to L5 | A′ (G0, G3) | 100 % of `moddle-type` entries bijection-proven; unknown-namespace fixtures retained | (A′ budget; parity-side 2–3) |
| L2a — Commands & rules core | Generic `command`/`rule` core (diagram-js commands, `elements.*`, transaction/inverse semantics, `commandStack.*` lifecycle) | L1 | 100 % of P0 generic `command`/`rule` entries differential-green; undo/redo round-trips equal | 3–4 |
| L2b — Containers, lanes, subprocess | `lane.*`, collapse/drilldown, subprocess planes and their behaviours (`ToggleElementCollapseBehaviour`, `SubProcessPlaneBehavior`, lane behaviours) | L2a | Container fixtures differential-green, including label/layout side effects (`LayoutConnectionBehavior`, `AdaptiveLabelPositioningBehavior` verdicts compared as geometry) | 2–3 |
| L2c — Replace, copy-paste, space tool | `BpmnReplace` semantics behind `replace-menu` 74, copy-paste, `spaceTool`; BPMN-specific commands (`element.update*`, `element.setColor`, `canvas.updateRoot`) | L2a | Replace/copy/space scenarios differential-green from each offering element type | 2–3 |
| L3 — Scene, camera, hit (complete diagram-canvas) | `api` canvas subset, selection/marquee, paint-order/hit completion; route adapter skeleton (§5.7) with the camera conversion in one place | L2a (can start on L1 fixtures) | Point/region/lasso selection verdicts equal on fixtures; camera events equal in the persisted (origin) reading on both adapters | 4–6 |
| L4 — Interaction lifecycle | drag/create/connect/bendpoints/resize/space-tool/snapping/auto-place/align/distribute/ordering/drilldown/label-editing/copy-paste/search (`module` + `event` + `keyboard` subsets) | L2a–L2c, L3 | 100 % of P0, ≥90 % of P1 interaction entries green; event-log comparison on all scripted scenarios | 10–16 |
| L5 — BPMN rendering | `renderer` 58 on the route-1 draw layer (or route-2 SVG nodes), explicit-`dc:Bounds` sizing (§5.7), markers/icons, text measurement decision implemented, `saveSVG` synthesised from the scene | L3; font-metric decision (§11) | Geometry comparison green on all renderer fixtures (bounds/waypoints at ±1 px, labels once the metric is pinned); pixel secondaries within per-fixture thresholds; `saveSVG` output compared as synthesised SVG, never as live-DOM serialisation | 6–10 |
| L6 — Chrome (DS components) | `palette` 15, `context-pad` 19, `replace-menu` 74, `popup-menu` 17 as DS components; editor shell, minimap/outline, overlays | L4, L5; E for hosting contract | Every menu entry reachable and differential-green from each offering element type | 6–9 |
| L7 — Route decision & (route 2 only) capitalisation | Owner decision on §6 evidence (L0 CSP + L4/L5 route measurements); if route 2 was taken: re-implement planes 4–5 on `@sentropic/graph` through the §5.7 adapter, retire translation layer | L0 (decision input), L6 (capitalisation) | Decision recorded; (route 2) full harness green on graph with the xyflow path removed | decision: 1; capitalisation: 8–14 |
| L8 — Public API, config, examples | `api` 437 service surface (viewer/modeler facades plus every didi service class), `config` 41, `example` 26 as acceptance scenarios (embedding/theming/i18n/overlays/minimap behaviours productised or explicitly refused; the 3 upstream tombstones stay excluded, §3.1) | L6 | Facade differential-green; every example either green or carrying a recorded refusal with owner | 4–7 |
| L9 — Hardening | a11y/keyboard completeness, reduced-motion, large-model performance, error/empty states, docs & migration path from bpmn-canvas | L8 | Full inventory: 100 % P0/P1 proven, P2 proven or explicitly waived per entry; bpmn-canvas cutover decision prepared | 4–6 |

Programme reading: L1–L6 are the parity core (~35–55 person-weeks plus the A′ budget, same judgement basis); route 2 adds L7 capitalisation later while (possibly) shortening L4–L6 — the L0/L4 measurements exist to price that trade with evidence instead of preference. No lot rewrites `.track/`; the conductor records lot openings/closures.

## 10. Licence and clean room

- bpmn-js is distributed under the bpmn.io licence: MIT terms **plus** the watermark clause — the source displaying the bpmn.io watermark linking to https://bpmn.io must not be removed or changed, and the watermark must stay fully visible when the software is used in a website or application (`RE:bpmn-js/LICENSE`, watermark paragraph). diagram-js is MIT (`RE:diagram-js/LICENSE`). bpmn-moddle is MIT (per its `LICENSE`).
- This programme **redistributes none of them**: it reimplements *behaviours* from observation (inputs → outputs, verdicts, geometry) in a clean room. Consequences: (a) no bpmn-js/diagram-js code, comments, file layout or asset (icons, CSS, fonts) is copied into DS packages — glyphs are redrawn from the BPMN 2.0 specification shapes and DS icon conventions; (b) the watermark clause has no object here, since no bpmn-js rendering ships — if a future lot *embeds* bpmn-js (route exceptions, spikes), the clause applies in full and the watermark stays; (c) `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` in this worktree gains entries only if a dependency is actually taken (e.g. xyflow, MIT, `RE:xyflow/LICENSE`, if route 2 is adopted) — decided at adoption, checked by the repository licensing gates.
- **PathMap prohibition (explicit).** The SVG path strings of `RE:bpmn-js/lib/draw/PathMap.js` (parameterised `d="M …"` icon geometry, covered by the bpmn-js licence including its watermark clause) must not be copied, transcribed or mechanically transformed into DS packages (including `packages/graph/src/shape-geometry.ts`). BPMN icon/marker geometry is produced clean-room from the BPMN 2.0 specification shapes or from DS icons under a documented MIT-only provenance; a lot that cannot produce an icon clean-room records it as missing instead of importing the path data.
- The inventory itself (behaviour descriptions, counts, `path:line` references) is factual documentation about the reference, written for interoperability; it contains no copied code. Scripts in `tools/re-bpmn-js/` are original Node code written for this lot.

## 11. Risks, pre-mortem and unknowns

### 11.1 Pre-mortem (it is 2027 and parity failed — why?)

1. **The model ate the schedule.** A′ generation (K-mechanisms, F1 alignment) slips, L2+ starve for fixtures, and "parity" quietly degrades into a renderer demo on hand-written models. Mitigation: L0 self-parity + L1 gate ordering; no rendering lot claims progress without model fixtures.
2. **Event-contract drift.** The target emits *almost* the reference events; integrations (undo, drilldown, search) break in ways model comparison cannot see. Mitigation: event-log comparison from L2 onward, dynamic-suffix logging (§3.3.1) in the harness.
3. **Route 2 becomes the product.** The xyflow vertical ships, capitalisation (L7) is never funded, and the DS carries a translation layer plus a DOM hit-test forever. Mitigation: L7 is in the plan from day one with its own gate; the facade (L8) is forbidden from leaking xyflow types — checked by type tests.
4. **Text measurement.** Label geometry depends on font metrics; WebGL text and DOM text disagree, fixtures flake, thresholds widen until the pixel arm is meaningless. Mitigation: font-metric decision before L5; geometry-first comparison (§8.3).
5. **The long tail of behaviours.** The 44 behaviour files (`RE:bpmn-js/lib/features/modeling/behavior/`, §5.2 count) hide most edge cases (boundary events, lanes, compensation). Command-level parity passes, real diagrams misbehave. Mitigation: behaviour-file accounting in L2/L4 acceptance; scenario corpus grows from every upstream spec file name, not from imagination.

### 11.2 Risk register (top items)

| Risk | Likelihood / impact [JUDGMENT] | Mitigation (lot) |
|---|---|---|
| F1 shape changes after the pinned `84c93dc` reading (A′ §4.2 drift already observed) | high / medium | Pin + alignment items F1-A1…A6; codec isolated in one package (L1) |
| CSP: strict `style-src-attr 'none'` vs interactive editors (xyflow violation already measured at load) | known / high for route 2 | L0 re-measure; capability-gated interactive surface, static SVG fallback (migration plan) |
| bpmn-js upstream moves (19.x) during the programme | medium / low | Pinned reference; re-run enumeration per upgrade — the guard reprices the delta mechanically |
| DS framework spread (Svelte pilot vs React/Vue/Angular parity) | medium / medium | Contract-first chrome (plane 5); per-framework proof only via executed scenarios (migration plan A4 discipline) |

### 11.3 Unknowns (`unknown` — no source in this workspace settles them)

- `unknown`: the `color` package's 3 type descriptors (source-gap, §3.3.2) — needs the `bpmn-in-color-moddle` files or a built bpmn-moddle dist.
- `unknown`: F1 final shape and the F1-A1…A6 alignments (bpmn-canvas unpublished branch) — owned by the A′ G0 gate.
- `unknown`: the standard-E (rendering by JSON) hosting contract for editor chrome — in specification elsewhere; L6 depends on its outcome for component shape, not for behaviour.
- `unknown`: font-metric source for WebGL text (self-hosted font + metrics table vs DOM measurement bridge) — decision required before L5.
- `unknown`: properties-panel ecosystem scope (several `bpmn-js-examples` entries demonstrate panel extensions; whether the DS offers an equivalent extension surface is a product decision, prepared in L8 as explicit refusals-or-scenarios).
- `unknown`: performance envelope demanded of the WebGL route (largest diagram size × interaction frame budget) — no figure exists in the workspace; L9 cannot gate without one.

